    {
      "filename": "Chapel at Mauna Ala - Royal Mausoleum, Honolulu, HI.jpg",
      "title": "File:Chapel at Mauna Ala - Royal Mausoleum, Honolulu, HI.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/be/Chapel_at_Mauna_Ala_-_Royal_Mausoleum%2C_Honolulu%2C_HI.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chapel_at_Mauna_Ala_-_Royal_Mausoleum,_Honolulu,_HI.jpg",
      "width": 1280,
      "height": 853,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Cliff",
      "description": "Chapel at Mauna Ala - Royal Mausoleum, Honolulu.\nQueen Lili‘uokalani had the building physically converted in 1904. Circular windows on two sides were cut out, where originally there were large crosses, and stained glass was installed. The Queen had arch-shaped windows which originally held the colors of the Hawaiian flag, in glass, removed and rose-colored stained glass installed.\nOn the outside of the building, concrete facing was put on top of the cut coral blocks, because the coral was actually crumbling off of the building.\nWith the passing of Prince Kuhio in 1922, his widow Princess Kahanu finished the conversion of the mausoleum building. The sanctuary area was created, the first koa paneling was lined along the walls, and the carved koa altar is installed. The flooring is of another native hardwood, the ohi‘a wood.",
      "dateCreated": "2007-11-30 11:43:45",
      "categories": [
        "CC-BY-2.0",
        "Chapels in Hawaii",
        "Flickr images uploaded by Flickr upload bot",
        "Photographs by Carl Clifford",
        "Royal Mausoleum of Hawaii",
        "Stone churches in Hawaii",
        "Taken with Canon EOS 5D"
      ],
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
